Source package: gnome-settings-daemon

power-sounds-follow-system-sounds.patch
    Lets the sounds the power plugin plays, the two for the power cable going in and out, the two
    for the lid opening and closing, and the one for a low battery, follow
    org.gnome.desktop.sound event-sounds, which is the System Sounds switch in Settings. The
    plugin hands them to libcanberra directly, and libcanberra applies that setting only in its
    GTK wrappers, so without this they play however the setting is set, and nothing turns them
    off.

    On this laptop the external monitor delivers power over USB-C, so connecting or disconnecting
    the monitor is a cable event as well, and sounded on every dock and undock.

    Only the sounds are held back. The low battery notification still appears, silently, and the
    lid still suspends.

The sounds are played in engine_charge_low, do_lid_open_action, do_lid_closed_action and
up_client_on_battery_cb in plugins/power/gsd-power-manager.c, which take the context from
gsd_application_get_ca_context in plugins/common/gsd-application.c. After a version bump, check
that all four still play them with no check of their own, and drop the patch once one is there.
The critical battery sounds in the same file, battery-caution and the alert loop it starts,
deliberately keep sounding whatever the setting says.

Check by hand after a rebuild and a new login, with System Sounds off in Settings:

  plug and unplug the charger or the monitor      should stay silent
  close the lid and open it again                 should stay silent, and still suspend
  switch System Sounds on and plug it again       should sound as it did before
